/* Full Lesson/Demonstration records extracted from the supplied DPO official-format DOCX files. */
const OFFICIAL_LESSON_RECORDS = {"1":{"no":1,"topic":"Fundamentals of Computers, Safe Working Practices, Safety Rules & Fire Extinguishers","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"1 (Computer Organization)","time":"2 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"10 August 2026 to 11 August 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["State the fundamentals and basic functional units of a computer.","Explain safe working practices and common safety rules/signs used in a computer lab.","Classify types of fire extinguishers and describe how each is used."],"materials":["White board & marker","Computer lab safety-sign chart","Fire extinguisher (demo model)/chart"],"review":"Ask trainees what they already know about computers from daily life (mobiles, laptops, cyber cafes).","motivation":"A DTP operator spends the entire working day at a computer; knowing the machine and lab safety rules protects both trainee and equipment.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A computer accepts data, processes it and gives output. Basic units: Input, CPU, Memory, Output.","Block diagram of a computer"],["Presentation / Development","General lab discipline; safety signs (Mandatory-blue, Prohibition-red, Warning-yellow, Information-green); fire extinguisher types (Water, Foam, CO2, Dry Powder, ABC) and their use per fire class.","Safety-sign colour chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees match safety signs to their meaning and identify the correct extinguisher for an electrical fire.","Extinguisher class labels"],["Consolidation / Checking","Oral quiz on functional units of a computer and fire-safety rules.","Recap key terms: CPU, Input, Output, Extinguisher class"]],"questions":["Why should water never be used on an electrical fire?","Name the four basic functional units of a computer.","List two general safety rules for a computer lab."],"summary":"A computer works through Input-Process-Output; lab safety and correct extinguisher use protect trainees and equipment.","assignment":"List five safety signs commonly found in a computer lab and state their meaning.","next":"Scope of the DTPO trade; introduction to computer components, hardware and software."},"2":{"no":2,"topic":"Scope of DTPO Trade; Computer Components; Hardware and Software","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"1 (Computer Organization)","time":"2 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"12 August 2026 to 13 August 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Describe the scope and career opportunities of the DTPO trade.","Identify the major components of a computer system.","Differentiate between hardware and software."],"materials":["White board & marker","Chart showing computer components","Sample hardware parts (RAM, mouse, keyboard)"],"review":"Recall the fundamentals of computers discussed in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding the trade's scope helps trainees connect each syllabus unit to real DTP jobs (print, publishing, design studios).","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","DTP operators work in printing presses, publishing houses, advertising and design studios.","List local press houses as examples"],["Presentation / Development","Computer components: Cabinet/CPU box, Motherboard, Monitor, Keyboard, Mouse, SMPS, Storage. Hardware = physical parts; Software = programs (System vs Application software).","Hardware vs Software table"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees classify a mixed list of items (mouse, MS Word, RAM, Photoshop) as hardware or software.","Classification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Review key terms; clarify doubts on hardware/software classification.","Recap: 3 examples each"]],"questions":["What is the difference between hardware and software?","Name three industries where a DTP operator can find employment.","Give one example each of system and application software."],"summary":"The DTPO trade spans print, publishing and design industries; every computer system is built of hardware components run by software.","assignment":"Prepare a short note on the scope of the DTPO trade in your local area.","next":"Function of motherboard components and various processors."},"3":{"no":3,"topic":"Function of Motherboard Components and Various Processors","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"1 (Computer Organization)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"14 August 2026 to 18 August 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify the major components mounted on a motherboard.","Explain the function of each motherboard component.","Describe the role of the processor and name common processor types."],"materials":["Motherboard (demo/cut model)","Chart of motherboard layout","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the hardware components identified in the previous lesson.","motivation":"The motherboard connects every other component; understanding it helps in troubleshooting and safe assembly.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","The motherboard is the main circuit board connecting CPU, memory, storage and peripherals.","Point out slots on an actual motherboard"],["Presentation / Development","Key components: CPU socket, RAM slots, PCIe slots, chipset, BIOS chip, power connectors, SATA ports. Processor executes instructions; families: Intel Core, AMD Ryzen.","Labelled motherboard chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees label a blank motherboard diagram with correct component names.","Blank diagram worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Oral quiz identifying components by shape/position on the chart.","Recap: CPU socket, RAM slot, chipset"]],"questions":["What is the function of the chipset on a motherboard?","Name two common processor families.","Why are RAM slots important on a motherboard?"],"summary":"The motherboard is the backbone connecting all hardware; the processor is the component that executes instructions.","assignment":"Draw and label a simple motherboard layout showing at least 6 components.","next":"Various Input/Output devices in use and their features."},"4":{"no":4,"topic":"Various Input/Output Devices in Use and Their Features","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"1 (Computer Organization)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"19 August 2026 to 21 August 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Classify computer devices as input or output devices.","Describe the features and uses of common input devices.","Describe the features and uses of common output devices."],"materials":["Actual input devices (keyboard, mouse, scanner)","Actual/pictured output devices (printer, monitor, speaker)","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the motherboard components and ports discussed in the previous lesson.","motivation":"DTP work depends heavily on scanners, printers and quality monitors; knowing their features helps in choosing the right device.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Input devices convert real-world data/actions into digital signals: keyboard, mouse, scanner, webcam, microphone.","Demonstrate scanning a sample photo"],["Presentation / Development","Output devices convert digital data into human-perceivable form: monitor, printer, speaker, projector. DPI/PPI and colour accuracy matter for DTP work.","Draft vs high-quality print comparison"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees list the input/output devices needed to prepare a photo-illustrated brochure.","Device checklist worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quick-fire classification quiz: input or output?","Recap: 5 input, 5 output devices"]],"questions":["What does DPI stand for and why does it matter for scanners/printers?","Classify a webcam and a projector as input or output devices.","Name two output devices used in a DTP workflow."],"summary":"Selecting the correct input/output device, based on resolution and colour accuracy, is essential for quality DTP output.","assignment":"Prepare a table listing 5 input and 5 output devices with one key feature of each.","next":"Introduction to different basic Accessories under MS Windows."},"5":{"no":5,"topic":"Introduction to Different Basic Accessories Under MS Windows","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"1 (Computer Organization)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"24 August 2026 to 27 August 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["List the basic accessories available under MS Windows.","Explain the purpose of each accessory (Notepad, Wordpad, Paint, Calculator, Snipping Tool).","Demonstrate how to open and close an accessory application."],"materials":["Computer with Windows OS","Projector/screen for demonstration"],"review":"Recall the difference between hardware and software covered earlier.","motivation":"Windows accessories are lightweight, always-available tools a DTP operator uses daily for quick notes, calculations and screenshots.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Accessories are found under Start Menu > Windows Accessories (or via Search).","Demonstrate opening Start Menu search"],["Presentation / Development","Common accessories: Notepad (plain text), Wordpad (rich text), Paint (basic drawing), Calculator, Snipping Tool (screenshots).","Open each accessory briefly"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees open three different accessories and state one use of each.","Accessory-use matching worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Review which accessory to use for a given task scenario.","Recap: Snip = screenshot, Notepad = quick text"]],"questions":["Where are Windows accessories usually located?","Name one practical DTP use of the Snipping Tool.","Which accessory would you use for a quick unformatted note?"],"summary":"Windows accessories are lightweight built-in tools that support everyday DTP tasks like notes, screenshots and quick calculations.","assignment":"List five MS Windows accessories with one practical use for each in DTP work.","next":"Introduction of the basic features of a document file."},"6":{"no":6,"topic":"Introduction to the Basic Features of a Document File","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"1 (Computer Organization)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"28 August 2026 to 31 August 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Define what a document file is.","List the basic features common to word-processor document files.","Identify common document file extensions (.doc, .docx, .odt, .rtf)."],"materials":["Computer with MS Word/Wordpad installed","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the Windows accessories covered in the previous lesson, including Wordpad.","motivation":"Most DTP output starts life as a document file; knowing its structure avoids formatting/compatibility problems later.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A document file stores formatted text, images, tables and layout information created by a word processor.","Open a sample .docx file"],["Presentation / Development","Basic features: fonts, styles, headers/footers, embedded images, tables, page setup. Common extensions: .doc/.docx, .odt, .rtf.","Save-As dialog format list"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees save the same file in two different document formats and compare.","Format comparison worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on matching extensions to their originating application.","Recap: .docx = Word, .odt = Writer"]],"questions":["What is a document file?","Name two features a document file supports that a plain text file does not.","Which extension does MS Word use by default?"],"summary":"A document file stores rich formatting information; recognising its common extensions avoids compatibility issues.","assignment":"List four document file extensions and one application that creates each.","next":"Discuss the difference between document file and text file."},"7":{"no":7,"topic":"Difference Between Document File and Text File","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"1 (Computer Organization)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"1 September 2026 to 3 September 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Differentiate between a document file and a plain text file.","Identify situations where each file type is appropriate.","Recognise common text file extensions (.txt, .csv, .log)."],"materials":["Computer with Notepad and Wordpad/MS Word","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the basic features of a document file discussed previously.","motivation":"Choosing the wrong file type for a task (e.g. using Word for a data-log) can cause compatibility and size problems.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A text file (.txt) stores only unformatted characters - no fonts, colours or embedded objects; very small file size.","Open a .txt file in Notepad"],["Presentation / Development","A document file stores rich formatting and is larger in size. Use text files for code/logs, document files for letters/brochures needing formatting.","Compare file sizes side by side"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees create the same content as both .txt and .docx and compare size and appearance.","Size comparison worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion: when would you deliberately choose a text file over a document file?","Recap: text = data, document = formatted output"]],"questions":["Why is a .txt file usually much smaller than a .docx file?","Give one situation where a text file is preferred over a document file.","Name two common text-file extensions."],"summary":"Text files are compact and unformatted; document files are richer but larger - choose based on the task at hand.","assignment":"State two situations where a text file is preferred over a document file, with reasons.","next":"Give the basic idea about image file including BMP and other formats."},"8":{"no":8,"topic":"Basic Idea About Image Files Including BMP and Other Formats","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"1 (Computer Organization)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"4 September 2026 to 7 September 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Define what a raster/bitmap image file is.","Describe the BMP format and its characteristics.","Identify other common image formats (JPEG, PNG, GIF, TIFF) and their typical uses."],"materials":["Computer with MS Paint / image viewer","Sample images in different formats","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the difference between document and text files discussed in the previous lesson.","motivation":"DTP work involves handling many image formats for print and web; picking the wrong one affects quality and file size.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A raster/bitmap image is made of a grid of pixels, each storing colour information; resolution measured in DPI/PPI.","Zoom into a sample image to show pixels"],["Presentation / Development","BMP stores image data with little/no compression (large file, no quality loss). JPEG (compressed photos), PNG (transparency, web), GIF (animation), TIFF (print/press quality).","Save one image in 3 formats and compare"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees save one image in BMP, JPEG and PNG and compare file size and quality.","Size/quality comparison table"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz matching image format to its best typical use case.","Recap: BMP=uncompressed, JPEG=photo, PNG=web/transparency"]],"questions":["Why is a BMP file usually much larger than a JPEG of the same image?","Which image format supports transparency, and where is it commonly used?","Name a format best suited for high-quality print/press work."],"summary":"Different image formats trade off quality, compression and transparency support - choosing correctly matters for print vs web DTP output.","assignment":"Prepare a short comparison table of BMP, JPEG, PNG and TIFF formats with one typical use for each.","next":"Introduction of Typing tutor software and its different options to improve typing speed."},"9":{"no":9,"topic":"Introduction to Typing Tutor Software and Its Options to Improve Typing Speed","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"3 (MS Office - MS Word, Excel & PowerPoint)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"8 September 2026 to 10 September 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the purpose of a typing tutor software.","Identify the different options/modes available in a typing tutor.","Describe how regular practice improves typing speed and accuracy."],"materials":["Computer with typing tutor software","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the MS Paint drawing skills covered in the previous unit.","motivation":"Fast, accurate typing is the foundation skill for every DTP task that follows in this course.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A typing tutor is software that teaches touch typing through structured, graded lessons.","List benefits: speed, accuracy, reduced strain"],["Presentation / Development","Common options: lesson mode, timed test mode, custom drills, progress reports, difficulty levels.","Show a typing tutor's main menu"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees complete a beginner lesson and note their WPM/accuracy score.","Sample progress report"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discuss which typing tutor option is most useful for a beginner vs an advanced user.","Recap: WPM, accuracy %"]],"questions":["What does WPM stand for?","Name two modes commonly found in typing tutor software.","Why is accuracy prioritised over speed for beginners?"],"summary":"Typing tutor software builds touch-typing skill through structured practice, tracked by speed and accuracy metrics.","assignment":"Complete two typing tutor lessons and record your WPM and accuracy for each.","next":"What is MS Office; brief introduction to MS Word and its starting steps; various screen components."},"10":{"no":10,"topic":"MS Office Overview; Introduction to MS Word and Its Screen Components","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"3 (MS Office - MS Word, Excel & PowerPoint)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"11 September 2026 to 16 September 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["State what MS Office is and list its major applications.","Describe the starting steps to open MS Word.","Identify the various screen components of the MS Word window."],"materials":["Computer with MS Office installed","LCD projector"],"review":"Recall the typing skills practised using the typing tutor.","motivation":"MS Word is the primary tool for most DTP document-preparation tasks - knowing its layout speeds up every later lesson.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","MS Office is a suite of productivity applications: Word (documents), Excel (spreadsheets), PowerPoint (presentations), etc.","List MS Office applications"],["Presentation / Development","Starting Word via Start Menu/shortcut; screen components: Title Bar, Ribbon, Quick Access Toolbar, Document area, Status Bar.","Labelled Word window diagram"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees open Word and point out each screen component on their own system.","Component identification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz matching component names to their location on screen.","Recap: Ribbon, QAT, Status Bar"]],"questions":["Name three applications included in MS Office.","What is displayed on the Status Bar in MS Word?","How do you open MS Word from the Start Menu?"],"summary":"MS Office is a suite of applications; MS Word's screen is organised into the Ribbon, Quick Access Toolbar, Document area and Status Bar.","assignment":"Label a printed screenshot of the MS Word window with the names of at least 5 components.","next":"Explain how to create a new document, save and print."},"11":{"no":11,"topic":"Creating a New Document; Saving and Printing","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"3 (MS Office - MS Word, Excel & PowerPoint)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"17 September 2026 to 19 September 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the steps to create a new document in MS Word.","Describe the process of saving a document in different formats.","Explain the steps to print a document."],"materials":["Computer with MS Word installed"],"review":"Recall the MS Word screen components covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Correctly saving and printing work is essential - lost or wrongly formatted files cause real workplace problems.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A new document is created via File > New > Blank Document.","New Document dialog"],["Presentation / Development","Save vs Save As; common formats (.docx, .pdf, .rtf); Print dialog options (printer, copies, page range).","Save As format list; Print dialog"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees create, save in two formats, and preview-print a sample document.","Print Preview screenshot"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discuss when to use Save vs Save As.","Recap: Ctrl+S, Ctrl+P shortcuts"]],"questions":["What is the shortcut key to save a document?","Name two file formats a Word document can be saved as.","What does the Print Preview feature show?"],"summary":"Saving regularly and choosing the correct file format ensures work is not lost and is compatible with its intended use.","assignment":"Create a one-page document, save it in .docx and .pdf formats, and note the file-size difference.","next":"Concept of font style and colour scheme; editing, formatting text and document styles; using tables."},"12":{"no":12,"topic":"Font Style and Colour Scheme; Editing/Formatting Text; Document Styles; Tables","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"3 (MS Office - MS Word, Excel & PowerPoint)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"21 September 2026 to 23 September 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the concept of font style and colour scheme in document design.","Describe how to edit and format text and apply document styles.","Explain the basic use of tables inside a Word document."],"materials":["Computer with MS Word installed","Sample formatted documents"],"review":"Recall the document creation, saving and printing process from the previous lesson.","motivation":"Consistent font and colour choices make documents look professional - a core DTP skill.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Font style = typeface/weight (e.g. bold Calibri); colour scheme = coordinated set of colours used consistently.","Show good vs poor font/colour choices"],["Presentation / Development","Editing/formatting text via Home tab; built-in Styles gallery for headings/body text; inserting a basic table for data.","Styles gallery; sample table"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees apply a heading style and insert a small data table into a sample document.","Styled document sample"],["Consolidation / Checking","Review why consistent styles matter across a multi-page document.","Recap: Styles gallery location"]],"questions":["What is a 'style' in MS Word?","Why is a consistent colour scheme important in document design?","How do you insert a table into a Word document?"],"summary":"Applying consistent font styles, colour schemes and built-in Word Styles gives documents a professional, uniform appearance.","assignment":"Format a one-page document using at least two built-in styles and one table.","next":"Explain how to create styles in a document and save for future use; brief the mail merge processing."},"13":{"no":13,"topic":"Creating and Saving Custom Styles; Introduction to Mail Merge","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"3 (MS Office - MS Word, Excel & PowerPoint)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"24 September 2026 to 28 September 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain how to create a custom style and save it for future use.","Describe the overall process of mail merge in MS Word."],"materials":["Computer with MS Word and Excel installed","Sample data source"],"review":"Recall the built-in Styles gallery covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Custom styles save time across many documents; mail merge is essential for bulk correspondence like certificates and admit cards.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A custom style is created by formatting text and saving it as a New Style, reusable across documents.","New Style dialog"],["Presentation / Development","Mail merge combines a main document with a data source (Excel) using merge fields to generate personalised documents (letters, labels, envelopes).","Mail merge process flow diagram"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees create one custom style and outline the 5 basic mail-merge steps for a sample scenario.","Mail merge steps worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on the sequence of mail merge steps.","Recap: Select Recipients > Insert Fields > Preview > Finish & Merge"]],"questions":["How do you save a custom style for future use?","What are the two essential components of a mail merge?","Name one real-world use of mail merge in an institute."],"summary":"Custom styles streamline repeated formatting; mail merge automates generating many personalised documents from one template and a data source.","assignment":"List three institute documents that could be produced faster using mail merge.","next":"Features & Functions of Microsoft Excel, formulas and Functions, moving around in Excel."},"14":{"no":14,"topic":"Features & Functions of MS Excel; Formulas and Functions; Moving Around; Conditional Formatting; Linking Spreadsheet Data","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"3 (MS Office - MS Word, Excel & PowerPoint)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"29 September 2026 to 1 October 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Describe the main features and functions of Microsoft Excel.","Explain how to write basic formulas and functions and navigate a worksheet.","Explain conditional formatting and linking data between spreadsheets."],"materials":["Computer with Microsoft Excel installed","LCD projector"],"review":"Recall the mail merge and custom styles concepts covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Excel is used throughout DTP work for data lists (mailing lists, inventories, pricing) that feed into mail merge and publications.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Excel is a spreadsheet application organised into rows, columns and cells, used for data storage, calculation and analysis.","Show a blank worksheet grid"],["Presentation / Development","Basic formulas (=A1+B1) and functions (SUM, AVERAGE); navigation via Ctrl+Arrow keys, Name Box; Conditional Formatting highlights data visually; linking pulls live data between sheets/files.","Formula bar; Conditional Formatting example"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees write a SUM formula, apply conditional formatting to a data range, and create a simple link between two cells.","Sample worksheet with formulas"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on formula syntax and the purpose of conditional formatting.","Recap: = sign starts every formula"]],"questions":["What symbol must every Excel formula begin with?","Name one function used to total a range of numbers.","What is the purpose of linking spreadsheet data?"],"summary":"Excel's formulas, functions, conditional formatting and data-linking features make it a powerful tool for organising and analysing DTP-related data.","assignment":"Create a worksheet with 10 sample records, apply a SUM formula and one conditional formatting rule.","next":"Introduction to PowerPoint and its advantages. Creating slide shows; fine-tuning presentations."},"15":{"no":15,"topic":"Introduction to PowerPoint and Its Advantages; Creating Slide Shows; Fine-Tuning Presentations","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"3 (MS Office - MS Word, Excel & PowerPoint)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"3 October 2026 to 6 October 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain what PowerPoint is and its advantages for presentations.","Describe the process of creating a slide show.","Explain techniques for fine-tuning a presentation."],"materials":["Computer with Microsoft PowerPoint installed","LCD projector"],"review":"Recall the Excel formulas, functions and conditional formatting covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"PowerPoint is used to pitch designs and present client work - a key communication skill for a DTP operator.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","PowerPoint is presentation software used to create slide shows combining text, images, charts and multimedia. Advantages: visual impact, easy editing, reusable templates.","Show a sample finished presentation"],["Presentation / Development","Creating a slide show: choose layout, add content, apply a theme, arrange slide order. Fine-tuning: consistent fonts/colours, appropriate animations, rehearsed timing, spell-check.","Slide Sorter view; Design tab themes"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees create a 3-slide presentation on a given topic and fine-tune its design consistency.","Sample 3-slide deck"],["Consolidation / Checking","Peer review of presentations for clarity and consistency.","Recap: theme, layout, fine-tuning checklist"]],"questions":["Name two advantages of using PowerPoint for presentations.","What does 'fine-tuning' a presentation typically involve?","Which view is used to rearrange slide order easily?"],"summary":"PowerPoint enables visually engaging presentations; careful slide creation and fine-tuning (consistency, timing, proofing) ensure a professional result.","assignment":"Create a 3-slide presentation introducing yourself, applying a consistent theme and at least one animation.","next":"Networks - necessity and advantages of networking."},"16":{"no":16,"topic":"Necessity and Advantages of Networking; Client-Server and Peer-to-Peer Concepts; Network Topologies","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"4 (Networking)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"7 October 2026 to 9 October 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the necessity and advantages of computer networking.","Differentiate between client-server and peer-to-peer networking.","Identify common network topologies."],"materials":["White board & marker","Network topology chart"],"review":"Recall the PowerPoint presentation skills covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Networking allows resource sharing (printers, files, internet) - essential for any modern DTP workplace.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A network connects computers to share resources and communicate. Advantages: resource sharing, communication, centralized data.","List benefits: sharing, communication, backup"],["Presentation / Development","Client-server: dedicated server provides services to clients. Peer-to-peer: all computers share resources equally. Topologies: Bus, Star, Ring, Mesh.","Topology diagrams chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees identify the topology used in the lab network and classify it as client-server or peer-to-peer.","Lab network diagram"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz matching topology names to their diagrams.","Recap: Star = most common in labs"]],"questions":["What is the main advantage of a client-server network over peer-to-peer?","Name two common network topologies.","Why is networking necessary in a DTP workplace?"],"summary":"Networking enables resource sharing and communication; understanding client-server vs peer-to-peer and topologies is fundamental to working in any networked lab.","assignment":"Draw and label two different network topologies with one advantage of each.","next":"Introduction to LAN, WAN and MAN."},"17":{"no":17,"topic":"Introduction to LAN, WAN and MAN","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"4 (Networking)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"12 October 2026 to 14 October 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Define LAN, WAN and MAN.","Differentiate between LAN, WAN and MAN based on geographic coverage and use."],"materials":["White board & marker","Map/chart showing network coverage areas"],"review":"Recall the network topologies discussed in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Knowing network types helps trainees understand how their institute's lab network connects to the wider internet.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","LAN (Local Area Network) covers a small area (lab/building); MAN (Metropolitan Area Network) covers a city; WAN (Wide Area Network) covers a large geographic area (country/globe).","Coverage-area comparison chart"],["Presentation / Development","Examples: LAN = institute lab; MAN = city cable network; WAN = the Internet. Discuss speed and cost trade-offs.","Real-world examples list"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees classify given examples (home WiFi, internet, city broadband) as LAN/MAN/WAN.","Classification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Oral quiz on coverage area of each network type.","Recap: LAN<MAN<WAN in size"]],"questions":["What does LAN stand for and what area does it typically cover?","Give one real-world example of a WAN.","Which network type would a single computer lab typically use?"],"summary":"LAN, MAN and WAN differ mainly by geographic coverage, from a single building to the entire globe.","assignment":"List three examples each of a LAN, MAN and WAN found in daily life.","next":"Network components: Modem, Hub, Switch, Router, Bridge, Gateway; Network cables; wireless networks and Bluetooth technology."},"18":{"no":18,"topic":"Network Components (Modem, Hub, Switch, Router, Bridge, Gateway); Cables; Wireless Networks and Bluetooth","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"4 (Networking)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"15 October 2026 to 17 October 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify common network hardware components and their functions.","Describe types of network cables used in wired networks.","Explain wireless networking and Bluetooth technology."],"materials":["Sample network devices (router, switch, modem, if available)","Network cable samples","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the LAN, WAN and MAN concepts covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Recognising network hardware helps trainees troubleshoot connectivity issues in a DTP lab.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Modem converts digital-analog signals; Hub broadcasts to all ports; Switch sends data to a specific device; Router connects networks; Bridge/Gateway connect different network types.","Device function comparison table"],["Presentation / Development","Cable types: Twisted Pair (Ethernet), Coaxial, Fibre Optic. Wireless networks use radio waves (WiFi); Bluetooth is a short-range wireless technology for device pairing.","Cable types chart; WiFi vs Bluetooth comparison"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees identify sample devices/cables and state their function.","Device identification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz matching device names to their functions.","Recap: Router = connects networks"]],"questions":["What is the difference between a Hub and a Switch?","Name one type of network cable.","How does Bluetooth differ from WiFi in terms of range?"],"summary":"Network hardware (modem, hub, switch, router) and cabling/wireless technologies work together to connect and route data between devices.","assignment":"Prepare a table listing 5 network devices with one function of each.","next":"Introduction to WWW, concept of internet, web browsers, internet servers and search engines."},"19":{"no":19,"topic":"Introduction to WWW; Concept of Internet, Web Browsers, Internet Servers and Search Engines","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"4 (Networking)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"19 October 2026 to 26 October 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the concept of the Internet and the World Wide Web.","Describe the role of web browsers and internet servers.","Explain how search engines work."],"materials":["Computer with internet access","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the network devices and cabling covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"The Internet and WWW are the backbone of research, communication and online publishing work.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","The Internet is a global network of networks; the WWW is a system of linked documents (webpages) accessed via the internet.","Internet vs WWW diagram"],["Presentation / Development","Web browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge) render webpages; internet/web servers host website content; search engines (Google, Bing) index and retrieve relevant pages for a query.","Client-server-search engine flow diagram"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees perform a search, open the resulting webpage, and identify the browser and approximate hosting server type.","Search-to-webpage flow worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on the difference between Internet and WWW, and the role of a search engine.","Recap: WWW is part of the Internet"]],"questions":["What is the difference between the Internet and the World Wide Web?","Name two popular web browsers.","How does a search engine help users find information?"],"summary":"The Internet is the global network infrastructure; the WWW runs on top of it via browsers, servers and search engines to deliver information to users.","assignment":"Write a short note explaining how a search engine finds and ranks webpages for a given query.","next":"Concepts of Domain Naming System and e-mail communication."},"20":{"no":20,"topic":"Concepts of Domain Naming System and E-mail Communication","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"4 (Networking)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"27 October 2026 to 31 October 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the Domain Naming System (DNS) and its role.","Describe the process of email communication."],"materials":["Computer with internet access","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the WWW, browsers and search engines covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding DNS and email helps trainees troubleshoot connectivity and communicate professionally online.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","DNS translates human-readable domain names (e.g. google.com) into IP addresses that computers use to locate servers.","DNS lookup process diagram"],["Presentation / Development","Email communication: SMTP for sending, POP3/IMAP for receiving; structure of an email address (user@domain); email etiquette.","Email address structure diagram"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees trace a sample domain name to its IP address using a command-line tool (nslookup/ping), and compose a properly formatted email.","nslookup command demo"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on DNS function and email protocol names.","Recap: DNS = 'phonebook of the internet'"]],"questions":["What does DNS stand for and what is its function?","Name one protocol used to send email and one used to receive email.","What are the two main parts of an email address?"],"summary":"DNS translates domain names into IP addresses, while SMTP/POP3/IMAP protocols enable sending and receiving email across the internet.","assignment":"Use a command-line tool to look up the IP address of three different websites and note the results.","next":"Introduction to video chatting tools and social networking concepts."},"21":{"no":21,"topic":"Introduction to Video Chatting Tools and Social Networking Concepts","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"4 (Networking)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"2 November 2026 to 6 November 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify common video chatting tools and their features.","Explain the concept and appropriate use of social networking."],"materials":["Computer with internet access and webcam","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the DNS and email communication concepts covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Video chatting and social networking are widely used for professional communication and portfolio-building in the design industry.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Video chatting tools (Zoom, Skype, Google Meet) allow real-time audio-video communication over the internet.","Video chat tool logos/features"],["Presentation / Development","Social networking platforms (LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook) connect users and allow content sharing; professional use includes portfolio showcasing and networking.","Platform-use comparison table"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees compare features of two video chatting tools and identify one platform suited for professional portfolio sharing.","Comparison worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on responsible and professional social media use.","Recap: privacy settings, professional tone"]],"questions":["Name two video chatting tools.","Which social networking platform is most suited for professional portfolio sharing, and why?","What is one privacy precaution to take when using social media?"],"summary":"Video chatting and social networking tools support professional communication and portfolio visibility when used responsibly.","assignment":"Create a short professional bio suitable for a LinkedIn-style profile.","next":"Concept on various network security protocols."},"22":{"no":22,"topic":"Concept of Various Network Security Protocols","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"4 (Networking)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"7 November 2026 to 16 November 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the purpose of network security protocols.","Identify common security protocols (WPA2/WPA3, HTTPS, firewall)."],"materials":["White board & marker","Sample router security settings screenshot"],"review":"Recall the video chatting and social networking concepts covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding security protocols protects both personal data and institute resources from cyber threats.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Network security protocols protect data during transmission and control access to a network.","Common threats overview"],["Presentation / Development","WPA2/WPA3 secure WiFi networks; HTTPS encrypts web traffic (padlock icon); Firewalls filter incoming/outgoing traffic based on rules.","Protocol function comparison table"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees check whether a sample website uses HTTPS and review the WiFi security type on a sample router screenshot.","HTTPS padlock identification"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on protocol names and their primary function.","Recap: HTTPS = secure web browsing"]],"questions":["What does the padlock icon in a browser's address bar indicate?","Name a secure WiFi encryption standard.","What is the function of a firewall?"],"summary":"Security protocols like WPA2/WPA3, HTTPS and firewalls work together to protect data and restrict unauthorized network access.","assignment":"Check three websites you commonly visit and note whether each uses HTTPS.","next":"Scanner: how to scan on a Windows computer and different scanner settings."},"23":{"no":23,"topic":"How to Scan on a Windows Computer","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"5 (Scanner and Printer)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"17 November 2026 to 21 November 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the steps involved in scanning a document on a Windows computer.","Identify the software/apps used for scanning on Windows."],"materials":["Computer with scanner installed","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the network security protocols covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Scanning is a daily task for a DTP operator digitising client artwork, photographs and reference documents.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Windows provides built-in apps (Windows Scan, Fax and Scan) for scanning; manufacturer software may offer more control.","Windows Scan app icon"],["Presentation / Development","Steps: connect scanner, open scanning app, select scanner, choose file type/location, preview, scan, save.","Step-by-step scan flow diagram"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees scan a sample document using the Windows Scan app and save it to a chosen folder.","Scan-and-save demo"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on the sequence of steps to scan a document.","Recap: connect > select > preview > scan > save"]],"questions":["Name a built-in Windows app used for scanning.","What should be done before starting the actual scan?","Where can the scanned file be saved?"],"summary":"Windows offers built-in and manufacturer scanning software; following a consistent step sequence ensures a properly saved, usable scan.","assignment":"Write the step-by-step sequence to scan and save a document using Windows Scan.","next":"Different settings of scanner; how to print from a USB or wirelessly-connected printer."},"24":{"no":24,"topic":"Different Scanner Settings; Printing from a USB or Wirelessly-Connected Printer","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"5 (Scanner and Printer)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"23 November 2026 to 30 November 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain different scanner settings (resolution, colour mode, file format).","Describe the process of printing from a USB or wirelessly-connected printer."],"materials":["Computer with scanner and printer installed","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the basic scanning process covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Correct scanner settings affect print/publication quality, while flexible printer connections (USB/wireless) suit different lab setups.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Scanner settings: resolution (DPI) affects detail/file size; colour mode (colour/grayscale/B&W); file format (JPEG/PDF/TIFF).","Settings comparison chart"],["Presentation / Development","USB printing: direct cable connection, plug-and-play driver install. Wireless printing: printer joins WiFi network, added via IP/discovery in Windows.","USB vs wireless connection diagram"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees compare scan output at two different DPI settings and connect to a printer via USB and wirelessly.","DPI comparison samples"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on choosing appropriate DPI for different document types.","Recap: higher DPI = more detail, larger file"]],"questions":["What does DPI control in a scan?","What is required to add a wirelessly-connected printer in Windows?","Name one advantage of USB printer connection over wireless."],"summary":"Scanner settings like resolution and colour mode determine scan quality, while USB and wireless connections offer different setup trade-offs for printing.","assignment":"List the DPI setting you would use for (a) a text document scan and (b) a high-quality photo scan, with reasons.","next":"Different settings of printer; sending and receiving fax; multifunction printers; modifying fax settings."},"25":{"no":25,"topic":"Printer Settings; Sending/Receiving Fax; Multifunction Printers; Modifying Fax Settings","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"5 (Scanner and Printer)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"1 December 2026 to 5 December 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Recall key printer settings covered previously.","Explain the process of sending and receiving a fax.","Describe the features of a multifunction printer and how to modify fax settings."],"materials":["Multifunction printer (print/scan/fax) if available","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the scanner settings and printer connection types covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Multifunction printers combining print, scan, copy and fax are common in publishing/press offices, so operators must know all their features.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Recap: paper size, print quality, colour mode, orientation settings on a printer.","Printer settings recap chart"],["Presentation / Development","Fax sends/receives documents over a phone line; multifunction printers combine print, scan, copy and fax in one device; fax settings include speed, resolution and auto-answer.","Multifunction printer diagram"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees identify the fax, scan, copy and print functions on a sample multifunction printer.","Function identification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on which settings apply to fax vs print functions.","Recap: fax = phone line, print = paper output"]],"questions":["What are the typical functions combined in a multifunction printer?","What does 'auto-answer' mean in fax settings?","Name one printer setting that also affects photo print quality."],"summary":"Multifunction printers combine printing, scanning, copying and faxing; understanding each function's settings ensures correct operation in a busy office.","assignment":"List the four core functions of a multifunction printer and one setting relevant to each.","next":"Benefits of using Adobe InDesign; features of Adobe PageMaker; concept of font style and colour scheme."},"26":{"no":26,"topic":"List the Benefits of Using Adobe InDesign","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"6 (Adobe InDesign)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"7 December 2026 to 11 December 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["List the key benefits of using Adobe InDesign for desktop publishing.","Relate these benefits to real-world publishing tasks."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe InDesign installed","Sample multi-page publications (magazine/brochure)"],"review":"Recall the printer settings and multifunction printer concepts covered in the previous unit.","motivation":"InDesign is the industry-standard layout tool for magazines, brochures and books - central to a DTP operator's career.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Adobe InDesign is a professional page-layout application used for multi-page publications like magazines, brochures and books.","Show sample InDesign-created publications"],["Presentation / Development","Benefits: precise typography control, master pages for consistency, robust colour management, seamless export to PDF/ePub/print.","Benefits list on chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees list which InDesign benefit would matter most for a 20-page magazine project.","Group discussion worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on InDesign's key benefits versus a basic word processor.","Recap: master pages, typography, export options"]],"questions":["Name two benefits of using InDesign over a basic word processor for layout work.","Why is master page support valuable for a multi-page magazine?","What export formats make InDesign useful for both print and digital publishing?"],"summary":"Adobe InDesign's strengths in typography, master pages, colour management and flexible export make it the standard tool for professional publishing.","assignment":"List three types of publications best suited to InDesign and explain why.","next":"Classify the features of Adobe PageMaker."},"27":{"no":27,"topic":"Classify the Features of Adobe PageMaker","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"6 (Adobe InDesign)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"14 December 2026 to 18 December 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Describe Adobe PageMaker as an earlier desktop publishing tool.","Classify its key features and limitations compared to modern tools."],"materials":["White board & marker","Reference material/screenshots of PageMaker (if available)"],"review":"Recall the benefits of Adobe InDesign discussed in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Knowing PageMaker's history helps trainees appreciate why InDesign became the industry standard and understand legacy files they may encounter.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Adobe PageMaker was one of the earliest desktop publishing applications, popular in the 1990s for basic page layout.","Brief history timeline"],["Presentation / Development","Features: basic text/graphic placement, simple master pages, limited colour management; largely discontinued and replaced by InDesign.","Feature list comparison"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees classify PageMaker features as 'basic' or 'advanced' compared to modern DTP needs.","Classification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion: why did InDesign replace PageMaker in the industry?","Recap: PageMaker discontinued ~2004"]],"questions":["What was Adobe PageMaker primarily used for?","Name one limitation of PageMaker compared to modern DTP software.","Approximately when was PageMaker discontinued?"],"summary":"Adobe PageMaker was an early, simpler desktop publishing tool whose limitations led to its replacement by more capable software like InDesign.","assignment":"Write a short note comparing one feature each of PageMaker and InDesign.","next":"Explain the concept of font style and colour scheme; compare the features of Adobe InDesign and Adobe PageMaker."},"28":{"no":28,"topic":"Concept of Font Style and Colour Scheme; Compare InDesign and PageMaker Features","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"6 (Adobe InDesign)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"19 December 2026 to 24 December 2026","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the concept of font style and colour scheme in page design.","Compare the features of Adobe InDesign and Adobe PageMaker."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe InDesign installed","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the classification of PageMaker features from the previous lesson.","motivation":"Consistent font and colour choices, backed by capable software, are what separate amateur layouts from professional publications.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Font style = typeface, weight and size choices; colour scheme = a coordinated palette applied consistently across a design.","Sample good vs poor colour scheme"],["Presentation / Development","InDesign offers advanced typography (OpenType features, paragraph/character styles) and robust colour management (spot/process colours) - far beyond PageMaker's basic capabilities.","Side-by-side feature comparison table"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees apply a font style and colour scheme to a sample layout in InDesign and note which PageMaker lacked.","Sample styled layout"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz comparing specific InDesign vs PageMaker features.","Recap: styles, spot colours are InDesign strengths"]],"questions":["What is meant by a 'colour scheme' in page design?","Name one typography feature InDesign offers that PageMaker did not.","Why is consistent font style important across a publication?"],"summary":"A considered font style and colour scheme are essential to professional design, and InDesign's advanced typography/colour tools make achieving this far easier than with PageMaker.","assignment":"Design a simple one-page flyer applying one font style and one coordinated colour scheme.","next":"Compare the features of different Adobe InDesign versions."},"29":{"no":29,"topic":"Compare the Features of Different Adobe InDesign Versions","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"6 (Adobe InDesign)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"28 December 2026 to 1 January 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify major Adobe InDesign versions and their release progression.","Compare key feature additions across recent InDesign versions."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe InDesign installed","Internet access for version history reference"],"review":"Recall the font style, colour scheme and InDesign-vs-PageMaker comparison from the previous lesson.","motivation":"Knowing version differences helps trainees troubleshoot file compatibility issues between different InDesign installations.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Adobe InDesign has evolved from standalone releases (CS series) to the current Creative Cloud (CC) subscription model with continuous updates.","InDesign version timeline"],["Presentation / Development","Recent versions add features like variable fonts, improved PDF export, Content-Aware Fit, and cloud-document collaboration.","Feature-addition comparison table"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees check their installed InDesign version (Help > About) and identify two features unique to recent versions.","About InDesign dialog"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on why keeping software updated matters for compatibility.","Recap: newer files may not open in older versions"]],"questions":["How can you check which version of InDesign is installed on a computer?","Name one feature added in recent versions of InDesign.","Why might an older version of InDesign fail to open a file created in a newer version?"],"summary":"InDesign has evolved considerably from the CS series to Creative Cloud, adding features that improve typography, export and collaboration over time.","assignment":"Check the InDesign version installed in the lab and list two features associated with that version.","next":"Summarize the system requirements for installing and working with formatting software."},"30":{"no":30,"topic":"System Requirements for Installing and Working with Formatting Software","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"6 (Adobe InDesign)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"2 January 2027 to 7 January 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify the minimum and recommended system requirements for DTP software.","Explain why adequate hardware specifications matter for smooth software performance."],"materials":["White board & marker","Sample system requirements chart (from software vendor website)"],"review":"Recall the InDesign version comparison covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding system requirements helps trainees troubleshoot slow performance and plan lab hardware upgrades.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","System requirements specify minimum/recommended RAM, processor, storage and OS version needed to run software smoothly.","Sample requirements table"],["Presentation / Development","DTP software (InDesign, Photoshop, Illustrator) is resource-intensive - insufficient RAM/CPU causes lag, especially with large image files.","RAM/CPU impact discussion"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees check the lab computers' specifications and compare against InDesign's recommended requirements.","System Properties check"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on what to upgrade first if a system struggles to run DTP software.","Recap: RAM often the first bottleneck"]],"questions":["What system specification most commonly causes DTP software to run slowly if insufficient?","Where can you check a computer's installed RAM and processor details?","Why do image-editing applications generally need more RAM than a word processor?"],"summary":"Meeting or exceeding recommended system requirements (RAM, CPU, storage) ensures DTP software runs smoothly, especially with large publication files.","assignment":"Check the specifications of your lab computer and compare them against Adobe InDesign's published system requirements.","next":"List the benefits of using Adobe Illustrator."},"31":{"no":31,"topic":"List the Benefits of Using Adobe Illustrator","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"7 (Adobe Illustrator)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"8 January 2027 to 14 January 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["List the key benefits of using Adobe Illustrator for vector design.","Relate these benefits to logo, illustration and typography work."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe Illustrator installed","Sample vector artwork examples"],"review":"Recall the system requirements for DTP formatting software covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Illustrator is the industry-standard tool for logos and vector graphics - core skills for a DTP operator's portfolio.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Adobe Illustrator is a vector graphics application used for logos, illustrations, icons and typography.","Show sample Illustrator artwork"],["Presentation / Development","Benefits: infinite scalability without quality loss, precise path-based editing, strong typography tools, wide export compatibility.","Benefits list chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees compare a vector logo scaled up against a bitmap logo scaled up, to see the quality difference.","Scale comparison demo"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on why vector graphics matter for logo design specifically.","Recap: scalability, precision, print-ready"]],"questions":["Why don't vector graphics lose quality when scaled up?","Name two types of design work best suited to Adobe Illustrator.","What advantage does path-based editing offer over pixel-based editing?"],"summary":"Adobe Illustrator's vector-based approach gives infinite scalability and precise editing, making it ideal for logos, icons and typography work.","assignment":"List three reasons a logo should be designed as a vector graphic rather than a bitmap image.","next":"Compare the features of different Adobe InDesign versions. (Illustrator scope: Logo Design, Graphic design, Illustration, Vector Graphics, Character design, UX design)"},"32":{"no":32,"topic":"Scope of Adobe Illustrator - Logo Design, Graphic Design, Illustration, Vector Graphics, Character Design and UX Design","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"7 (Adobe Illustrator)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"15 January 2027 to 18 January 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify the different design disciplines Adobe Illustrator supports.","Relate each discipline to a real-world DTP/design task."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe Illustrator installed","Sample work across each discipline"],"review":"Recall the general benefits of Illustrator discussed in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding Illustrator's full scope helps trainees see career pathways beyond just logo design.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Illustrator supports: Logo Design, Graphic Design (posters/ads), Illustration (artwork), Vector Graphics (icons/patterns), Character Design, and UX Design (wireframes/icons for apps).","List of six disciplines chart"],["Presentation / Development","Show one sample project from each discipline and discuss the skills/tools involved (Pen tool, Type tool, Symbols, Artboards).","Sample project gallery"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees match sample images to the correct discipline (logo/illustration/character/UX).","Matching worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on which discipline most interests trainees and why.","Recap: 6 disciplines list"]],"questions":["Name three design disciplines supported by Adobe Illustrator.","How might Illustrator be used in UX (user experience) design?","What is the difference between graphic design and illustration as disciplines?"],"summary":"Adobe Illustrator supports a wide range of design disciplines - from logos and illustrations to character and UX design - all built on the same vector toolset.","assignment":"Choose one Illustrator discipline (e.g. character design) and write a short note on the skills it requires.","next":"Discuss the benefits of using Adobe Photoshop and CorelDraw."},"33":{"no":33,"topic":"Overview of Illustrator's Application Areas (Recap and Consolidation)","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"7 (Adobe Illustrator)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"19 January 2027 to 21 January 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Consolidate understanding of Illustrator's core application areas.","Prepare to transition into raster-based tools (Photoshop) and CorelDraw."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe Illustrator installed","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the six design disciplines supported by Illustrator, covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"A clear recap consolidates vector-design skills before trainees move on to raster editing in Photoshop and CorelDraw.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Recap: Illustrator is vector-based, ideal for scalable, editable graphics across logo/illustration/icon/typography work.","Recap chart of Illustrator strengths"],["Presentation / Development","Preview upcoming units: Photoshop (raster/photo editing) and CorelDraw (another vector tool) will build on these design fundamentals with different strengths.","Illustrator vs Photoshop vs CorelDraw comparison"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees list one project type suited to Illustrator and one suited to a raster tool like Photoshop.","Comparison worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Open quiz reviewing all Illustrator topics covered in this unit.","Recap: full unit review"]],"questions":["What is the fundamental difference between vector tools (Illustrator) and raster tools (Photoshop)?","Name one project type better suited to a vector tool.","Name one project type better suited to a raster tool."],"summary":"Illustrator's vector approach complements raster tools like Photoshop; understanding when to use each is key to an efficient DTP workflow.","assignment":"Write a short comparison: for a company logo vs a photo-retouching project, which tool (Illustrator or Photoshop) would you use and why?","next":"Classify the features of CorelDraw and Adobe Photoshop."},"34":{"no":34,"topic":"Discuss the Benefits of Using Adobe Photoshop and CorelDraw","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"8 (Adobe Photoshop)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"22 January 2027 to 27 January 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["List the key benefits of using Adobe Photoshop for image editing.","List the key benefits of using CorelDraw for vector design.","Compare when each tool is the better choice."],"materials":["Computer with Photoshop and CorelDraw installed (if available)","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the design disciplines supported by Adobe Illustrator from the previous unit.","motivation":"Photoshop and CorelDraw are two more industry-standard tools every DTP operator must be comfortable switching between.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Photoshop is a raster (pixel-based) editor ideal for photo editing and complex image compositing.","Sample Photoshop photo-edit before/after"],["Presentation / Development","CorelDraw is a vector-based illustration tool similar to Illustrator, popular for sign-making, packaging and print layouts in many regional markets.","Sample CorelDraw vector artwork"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees classify sample tasks (photo retouch, business card design, poster with photos) by which tool suits each best.","Task classification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on raster vs vector strengths of each application.","Recap: Photoshop=raster/photos, CorelDraw=vector/print layouts"]],"questions":["Is Photoshop a raster or vector-based application?","Name one type of project where CorelDraw would be preferred over Photoshop.","Why might a designer need both Photoshop and CorelDraw for a single project?"],"summary":"Photoshop excels at raster photo editing while CorelDraw excels at vector illustration and print layout - many real projects use both together.","assignment":"List three real-world DTP tasks and state whether Photoshop, CorelDraw, or both would be used for each.","next":"Classify the features of CorelDraw and Adobe Photoshop."},"35":{"no":35,"topic":"Classify the Features of CorelDraw and Adobe Photoshop","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"8 (Adobe Photoshop)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"28 January 2027 to 30 January 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Classify Photoshop's core features (layers, filters, retouching tools).","Classify CorelDraw's core features (vector tools, colour management, page layout)."],"materials":["White board & marker","Feature comparison chart"],"review":"Recall the benefits comparison of Photoshop and CorelDraw from the previous lesson.","motivation":"Knowing which features belong to which application prevents confusion when switching between raster and vector workflows.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Photoshop core features: Layers, Filters, Retouching tools, Selection tools, Adjustment layers.","Photoshop feature list chart"],["Presentation / Development","CorelDraw core features: Vector drawing tools, Colour management (CMYK/Pantone), multi-page document layout, print-ready export.","CorelDraw feature list chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees sort a mixed list of features into 'Photoshop' or 'CorelDraw' columns.","Feature sorting worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz identifying which application a given feature belongs to.","Recap: Layers=Photoshop, Vector tools=CorelDraw"]],"questions":["Name two core features of Adobe Photoshop.","Name two core features of CorelDraw.","Which application would you use for non-destructive photo retouching, and why?"],"summary":"Photoshop's strengths centre on layered, pixel-based editing while CorelDraw's strengths centre on vector drawing and print-ready page layout.","assignment":"Create a two-column chart listing 5 features each for Photoshop and CorelDraw.","next":"Compare the features of various Adobe Photoshop and CorelDraw versions."},"36":{"no":36,"topic":"Compare the Features of Various Adobe Photoshop and CorelDraw Versions","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"8 (Adobe Photoshop)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"1 February 2027 to 3 February 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify major Adobe Photoshop version milestones.","Identify major CorelDraw version milestones and compare feature progression."],"materials":["Computer with internet access for version history reference","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the feature classification of Photoshop and CorelDraw from the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding version differences helps trainees troubleshoot file compatibility between labs using different software versions.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Photoshop has evolved from standalone CS releases to the Creative Cloud (CC) subscription model with continuous feature updates.","Photoshop version timeline"],["Presentation / Development","CorelDraw has similarly evolved through numbered versions (e.g. X7, X8, 2020, 2021+) adding features like enhanced typography and collaboration tools.","CorelDraw version timeline"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees check the installed version of each application (Help > About) and note one feature unique to that version.","About dialog check"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on file compatibility issues between different software versions.","Recap: newer files may not open in older versions"]],"questions":["How can you check the installed version of Photoshop or CorelDraw?","Why might a file created in a newer version fail to open in an older version?","Name one feature improvement seen in recent versions of either application."],"summary":"Both Photoshop and CorelDraw have evolved considerably over their version histories, and version awareness helps avoid file-compatibility problems.","assignment":"Check the Photoshop and CorelDraw versions installed in your lab and note the release year of each.","next":"Explain the concept of font style and colour scheme; introduction to the Tools panel."},"37":{"no":37,"topic":"Font Style and Colour Scheme; Introduction to the Tools Panel (Selection, Crop/Slice, Measuring, Retouching, Painting, Drawing/Type, Navigation)","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"8 (Adobe Photoshop)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"4 February 2027 to 6 February 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the concept of font style and colour scheme as applied within Photoshop.","Identify the major tool groups in Photoshop's Tools panel and their general purpose."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe Photoshop installed","Tools panel reference chart"],"review":"Recall the version comparison of Photoshop and CorelDraw from the previous lesson.","motivation":"A clear map of the Tools panel groups helps trainees quickly find the right tool during hands-on practical sessions.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Font style and colour scheme apply within Photoshop's Type tool and colour panels, just as in other DTP software, for consistent text design in composites.","Type tool + colour panel demo"],["Presentation / Development","Tools panel groups: Selection (Marquee/Lasso/Wand), Crop & Slice, Measuring (Ruler/Eyedropper), Retouching (Clone/Heal), Painting (Brush/Gradient), Drawing & Type (Pen/Type), Navigation (Hand/Zoom).","Labelled Tools panel chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees match each tool icon to its group name on a printed Tools panel chart.","Tools panel matching worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on which group a specific tool (e.g. Clone Stamp) belongs to.","Recap: 7 tool groups overview"]],"questions":["Name the seven major tool groups in the Photoshop Tools panel.","Which tool group does the Eyedropper belong to?","Why is understanding tool groupings useful when learning new tools quickly?"],"summary":"Photoshop's Tools panel is organised into clear functional groups - Selection, Crop/Slice, Measuring, Retouching, Painting, Drawing/Type and Navigation - each central to different editing tasks.","assignment":"Label a printed Tools panel diagram identifying at least 7 tools and their group.","next":"Introduction to Menu bar, Arrange, layout, Effects, Bitmap etc."},"38":{"no":38,"topic":"Introduction to Menu Bar, Arrange, Layout, Effects and Bitmap Options","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"8 (Adobe Photoshop)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"8 February 2027 to 10 February 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify the main menus in Photoshop's Menu bar and their general purpose.","Explain the concepts of Arrange, Layout, Effects and Bitmap mode."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe Photoshop installed","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the Tools panel groups covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Confident menu navigation speeds up every future Photoshop practical session.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Menu bar: File (open/save/export), Edit (undo/transform), Image (size/mode/adjustments), Layer, Select, Filter, View, Window.","Menu bar overview chart"],["Presentation / Development","Arrange controls window/panel layout; Layout relates to canvas/document setup; Effects (via Filter/Layer Style) add visual enhancements; Bitmap mode reduces an image to 1-bit black/white.","Arrange vs Layout vs Effects comparison"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees identify which menu they would use for five given tasks (e.g. resize canvas, add a filter, undo an action).","Task-to-menu matching worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on menu bar structure and Bitmap mode's characteristics.","Recap: Bitmap = 1-bit colour depth"]],"questions":["Which menu would you use to change an image's canvas size?","What is Bitmap mode in Photoshop, in terms of colour depth?","Name one menu used to apply visual effects/filters."],"summary":"Photoshop's Menu bar organises all major functions logically by task type; understanding Arrange, Layout, Effects and Bitmap mode builds menu fluency.","assignment":"List the menu bar location for 5 common Photoshop tasks (e.g. resize, undo, add filter, change mode, export).","next":"Create, format, edit and develop images using Photoshop Software."},"39":{"no":39,"topic":"Create, Format, Edit and Develop Images Using Photoshop Software","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"8 (Adobe Photoshop)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"11 February 2027 to 15 February 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the overall workflow of creating and developing an image in Photoshop.","Describe key formatting and editing steps used to develop a finished image."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe Photoshop installed","Sample source images"],"review":"Recall the Menu bar structure and Bitmap mode covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"This lesson consolidates the full image-development workflow trainees will use throughout their DTP career.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A typical image workflow: create/open a canvas, place source content, edit with tools/adjustments, format with layers/styles, and export the final image.","Workflow diagram: Create > Edit > Format > Export"],["Presentation / Development","Formatting includes resizing, cropping and colour correction; editing includes retouching and compositing; developing means refining the image over multiple passes towards the final result.","Sample image at multiple workflow stages"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees take a raw sample image through the full workflow: create, edit, format and export a finished version.","Multi-stage image development exercise"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on why working in stages (non-destructively) is better than editing directly on one flat image.","Recap: layered, staged workflow benefits"]],"questions":["What are the four broad stages of a typical Photoshop image-development workflow?","Why is non-destructive (layered) editing preferable to flattening an image early?","Name one formatting step typically performed in the early stage of the workflow."],"summary":"A structured create-edit-format-develop workflow, done non-destructively with layers, produces higher quality and more flexible final images.","assignment":"Take a sample photo through a full edit workflow (crop, colour-correct, retouch) and submit before/after versions.","next":"Compare Formatting and Design operations."},"40":{"no":40,"topic":"Compare Formatting and Design Operations","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"8 (Adobe Photoshop)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"16 February 2027 to 18 February 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Differentiate between formatting operations (technical adjustments) and design operations (creative choices).","Relate this distinction to Photoshop's tools and workflow."],"materials":["Computer with Adobe Photoshop installed","Sample formatted vs designed image comparison"],"review":"Recall the image-development workflow covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding the distinction between formatting and design operations helps trainees plan their editing approach efficiently.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Formatting operations = technical adjustments (resize, crop, resolution, colour mode) that prepare an image for a specific use.","Formatting operations examples"],["Presentation / Development","Design operations = creative choices (composition, colour palette, typography, effects) that shape the image's visual message.","Design operations examples"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees classify a list of Photoshop actions as either 'formatting' or 'design'.","Classification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on which operations typically come first in a real project workflow.","Recap: formatting often precedes design refinement"]],"questions":["What is the difference between a formatting operation and a design operation?","Is 'changing resolution for print' a formatting or design operation?","Why might formatting operations typically be performed before design refinement?"],"summary":"Formatting operations technically prepare an image, while design operations shape its creative outcome - both are necessary parts of a complete DTP workflow.","assignment":"Classify 8 given Photoshop actions as 'formatting' or 'design' and justify two of your choices.","next":"List the system requirements for installing and working with CorelDraw software."},"41":{"no":41,"topic":"List the System Requirements for Installing and Working with CorelDraw Software","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"9 (CorelDraw)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"20 February 2027 to 23 February 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify the minimum and recommended system requirements for CorelDraw.","Explain why adequate hardware matters for smooth CorelDraw performance."],"materials":["White board & marker","Sample system requirements chart (from CorelDraw's website)"],"review":"Recall the layer style operations covered at the end of the Photoshop unit.","motivation":"Knowing CorelDraw's requirements helps trainees troubleshoot lab performance and plan future hardware needs.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","System requirements specify minimum/recommended RAM, processor, storage and OS version for smooth CorelDraw operation.","Sample requirements table"],["Presentation / Development","CorelDraw, like other DTP software, benefits from sufficient RAM and a capable graphics card, especially for complex vector files with many objects.","RAM/GPU impact discussion"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees check the lab computers' specifications against CorelDraw's published requirements.","System Properties check"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on what to upgrade first if CorelDraw runs slowly.","Recap: RAM and GPU are common bottlenecks"]],"questions":["What system specification most commonly affects CorelDraw's performance with complex files?","Where can you check a computer's installed RAM and processor details?","Why might a graphics card matter for vector software like CorelDraw?"],"summary":"Meeting CorelDraw's recommended system requirements, particularly RAM and graphics capability, ensures smooth performance with complex vector projects.","assignment":"Check your lab computer's specifications and compare them against CorelDraw's published system requirements.","next":"Discuss the benefits of using CorelDraw."},"42":{"no":42,"topic":"Discuss the Benefits of Using CorelDraw","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"9 (CorelDraw)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"24 February 2027 to 26 February 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["List the key benefits of using CorelDraw for vector design and print layout.","Relate these benefits to real-world DTP tasks such as signage and packaging."],"materials":["Computer with CorelDraw installed","Sample CorelDraw-created designs"],"review":"Recall the system requirements for CorelDraw covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"CorelDraw is especially popular in the sign-making and packaging industries - a valuable skill for regional DTP career opportunities.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","CorelDraw is a vector illustration and page-layout application widely used for logos, packaging, signage and print layouts.","Show sample CorelDraw designs"],["Presentation / Development","Benefits: precise vector editing, strong colour management (CMYK/Pantone/spot colours), multi-page layout support, wide file-format compatibility for print vendors.","Benefits list chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees list which CorelDraw benefit matters most for a sign-making or packaging project.","Group discussion worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on CorelDraw's key benefits versus a basic drawing tool.","Recap: colour management, print compatibility"]],"questions":["Name two benefits of using CorelDraw for print-related design work.","Why is CorelDraw particularly popular in the sign-making industry?","What colour systems does CorelDraw support for accurate print colour?"],"summary":"CorelDraw's strong vector editing, colour management and print-format compatibility make it a preferred tool for signage, packaging and print layout work.","assignment":"List three types of businesses/industries that commonly use CorelDraw and explain why.","next":"Compare the features of various CorelDraw versions."},"43":{"no":43,"topic":"Compare the Features of Various CorelDraw Versions","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"9 (CorelDraw)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"1 March 2027 to 3 March 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify major CorelDraw version milestones.","Compare key feature additions across recent CorelDraw versions."],"materials":["Computer with CorelDraw installed","Internet access for version history reference"],"review":"Recall the benefits of CorelDraw discussed in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Knowing version differences helps trainees troubleshoot file compatibility between labs using different CorelDraw releases.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","CorelDraw has progressed through numbered versions (X3 through X8) and now annual releases (2018-present) with continuous feature updates.","CorelDraw version timeline"],["Presentation / Development","Recent versions add features like improved typography, symmetry drawing mode, non-destructive effects and better collaboration tools.","Feature-addition comparison table"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees check their installed CorelDraw version (Help > About) and identify one feature unique to recent versions.","About CorelDraw dialog"],["Consolidation / Checking","Discussion on why keeping software updated matters for compatibility.","Recap: newer files may not open in older versions"]],"questions":["How can you check which version of CorelDraw is installed on a computer?","Name one feature added in recent versions of CorelDraw.","Why might an older CorelDraw version fail to open a file from a newer version?"],"summary":"CorelDraw has evolved considerably across its version history, adding features that improve typography, effects and collaboration over time.","assignment":"Check the CorelDraw version installed in the lab and list two features associated with that version.","next":"Introduction to tools panel (Pick, shape, zoom, curve, smart drawing, rectangle, ellipse, object, text, interactive, eyedropper, outline and fill tools)."},"44":{"no":44,"topic":"Introduction to the CorelDraw Tools Panel","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"9 (CorelDraw)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"4 March 2027 to 6 March 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify the major tools in CorelDraw's Tools panel and their groupings.","Relate each tool group to typical design tasks."],"materials":["Computer with CorelDraw installed","Tools panel reference chart"],"review":"Recall the CorelDraw version comparison covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"A clear map of CorelDraw's Tools panel speeds up every future hands-on practical session.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Tools panel groups: Pick, Shape, Zoom, Curve, Smart Drawing, Rectangle, Ellipse, Object, Text, Interactive, Eyedropper, Outline and Fill tools.","Labelled Tools panel chart"],["Presentation / Development","Pick tool selects/transforms objects; Shape tool edits nodes; Curve tools draw freehand/Bezier lines; Interactive tools apply effects like blends and gradients.","Tool group function summary"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees match each tool icon to its group name on a printed Tools panel chart.","Tools panel matching worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on which group a specific tool (e.g. Eyedropper) belongs to.","Recap: 13 tool groups overview"]],"questions":["What is the function of the Shape tool in CorelDraw?","Which tool group would you use to apply an interactive gradient fill?","Name two tools used primarily for drawing curves."],"summary":"CorelDraw's Tools panel groups tools by function - selection, shape editing, drawing, text and fill - forming the foundation for all design work in the application.","assignment":"Label a printed CorelDraw Tools panel diagram identifying at least 8 tools and their group.","next":"Difference between Vector and Bitmap images, CMYK, RGB and Pantone colours."},"45":{"no":45,"topic":"Difference Between Vector and Bitmap Images; CMYK, RGB and Pantone Colours; Orientation, Paper Size, Bars","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"9 (CorelDraw)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"8 March 2027 to 11 March 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Differentiate between vector and bitmap (raster) images.","Explain CMYK, RGB and Pantone colour systems and their appropriate uses.","Recall document orientation, paper size and interface bars covered in demonstration work."],"materials":["Computer with CorelDraw installed","Sample vector and bitmap images"],"review":"Recall the CorelDraw Tools panel groups covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding vector vs bitmap and correct colour systems prevents costly print errors in professional DTP work.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Vector images are made of mathematical paths (scalable, editable); bitmap/raster images are made of pixels (fixed resolution).","Zoomed comparison: vector vs bitmap"],["Presentation / Development","RGB (Red-Green-Blue) is used for screens; CMYK (Cyan-Magenta-Yellow-Black) is used for print; Pantone provides standardised spot colours for exact colour matching.","Colour system comparison chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees identify sample images as vector or bitmap, and match a colour system to a given use-case (web banner vs printed brochure).","Classification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on when to use RGB vs CMYK vs Pantone.","Recap: RGB=screen, CMYK=print, Pantone=exact match"]],"questions":["Why do vector images not lose quality when scaled up, unlike bitmap images?","Which colour mode should be used for a document going to print?","What is the purpose of a Pantone colour in professional printing?"],"summary":"Vector graphics scale without quality loss while bitmaps are resolution-dependent; choosing the correct colour system (RGB, CMYK or Pantone) is essential for accurate screen and print output.","assignment":"Find one example each of a vector and a bitmap image on your computer and note the file format of each.","next":"What is Cloud servers and types of cloud server?"},"46":{"no":46,"topic":"What is a Cloud Server and Types of Cloud Servers?","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"10 (Cloud Computing)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"12 March 2027 to 16 March 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Define a cloud server and explain how it differs from a traditional physical server.","Classify the types of cloud servers (public, private, hybrid)."],"materials":["Computer with internet access","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the vector vs bitmap and colour system concepts covered in the previous unit.","motivation":"Cloud services are increasingly used in publishing workflows for storage, collaboration and hosting - essential modern DTP knowledge.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A cloud server is a virtual server running on shared infrastructure hosted by a cloud provider, accessed over the internet.","Cloud vs physical server diagram"],["Presentation / Development","Types: Public cloud (shared infrastructure, e.g. AWS/Azure), Private cloud (dedicated to one organisation), Hybrid cloud (mix of both).","Public/Private/Hybrid comparison chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees classify given scenarios (a startup using AWS, a bank's internal servers) by cloud type.","Scenario classification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on the defining characteristic of each cloud type.","Recap: public=shared, private=dedicated"]],"questions":["What is the main difference between a public and a private cloud?","Name one advantage of using a cloud server over a physical server.","Give an example of a hybrid cloud use case."],"summary":"Cloud servers provide flexible, internet-accessible computing resources, categorised as public, private or hybrid based on infrastructure ownership and sharing.","assignment":"List two advantages and one disadvantage of using public cloud servers.","next":"Introduction of Cloud storage and its types (Amazon, Google Drive & MS Azure Storage/OneDrive)."},"47":{"no":47,"topic":"Introduction to Cloud Storage and Its Types (Amazon, Google Drive, MS Azure/OneDrive)","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"10 (Cloud Computing)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"17 March 2027 to 19 March 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain the concept of cloud storage.","Identify popular cloud storage services and their key features."],"materials":["Computer with internet access","Sample cloud storage accounts (Google Drive, OneDrive)"],"review":"Recall the definition and types of cloud servers covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Cloud storage lets DTP operators access and share large publication files from anywhere, supporting remote and collaborative work.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Cloud storage lets users store files on remote servers, accessible via the internet from any device.","Cloud storage concept diagram"],["Presentation / Development","Popular services: Amazon S3 (developer-focused object storage), Google Drive (general file storage/collaboration), Microsoft OneDrive/Azure Storage (integrated with Windows/Office).","Service comparison chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees upload a sample file to Google Drive or OneDrive and share it with a classmate.","Upload and share demo"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz matching cloud storage services to their primary use case.","Recap: S3=developer, Drive/OneDrive=general use"]],"questions":["Name two popular cloud storage services.","What is one benefit of storing publication files in the cloud rather than only locally?","Which cloud storage service integrates most closely with Microsoft Office?"],"summary":"Cloud storage services like Google Drive, OneDrive and Amazon S3 enable flexible, remote access to files, supporting collaboration in modern DTP workflows.","assignment":"Upload a sample file to a cloud storage service of your choice and share the link with a partner.","next":"Definition, function and features of Web servers; types of web servers (Apache, IIS etc.)."},"48":{"no":48,"topic":"Definition, Function and Features of Web Servers; Types (Apache, IIS)","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"10 (Cloud Computing)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"20 March 2027 to 24 March 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Define what a web server is and explain its core function.","Identify common types of web server software (Apache, IIS) and their features."],"materials":["Computer with internet access","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the cloud storage services and their use cases covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding web servers helps trainees grasp how their hosted publications and websites are actually delivered to visitors.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","A web server is software (and the hardware running it) that stores, processes and delivers webpages to clients (browsers) on request.","Client-server request-response diagram"],["Presentation / Development","Apache HTTP Server is a popular open-source, cross-platform web server; Microsoft IIS is a web server built into Windows Server, tightly integrated with Microsoft technologies.","Apache vs IIS comparison chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees identify which web server software a sample website might be using, using an online checker tool.","Web server detection tool demo"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on the core function of a web server and the two named examples.","Recap: web server = stores + delivers webpages"]],"questions":["What is the core function of a web server?","Name two examples of web server software.","Which web server is tightly integrated with the Windows operating system?"],"summary":"A web server stores and delivers webpages to client browsers on request; Apache and IIS are two widely used examples with different platform strengths.","assignment":"Use an online tool to check which web server software two of your favourite websites use.","next":"What is Hosting; procedure of web hosting."},"49":{"no":49,"topic":"What is Hosting; Procedure of Web Hosting","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"10 (Cloud Computing)","time":"5 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"25 March 2027 to 30 March 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Define web hosting and explain its purpose.","Describe the general procedure for hosting a website."],"materials":["Computer with internet access","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the web server definitions and types covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding the hosting procedure ties together domain registration, DNS and file upload into one complete picture for publishing a website.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Web hosting is a service that provides server space and technology needed to make a website accessible via the internet.","Hosting concept diagram"],["Presentation / Development","Procedure: (1) choose a hosting provider/plan, (2) register/point a domain, (3) upload website files, (4) configure DNS, (5) test the live site.","Step-by-step hosting flow diagram"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees sequence a shuffled list of hosting steps into the correct order.","Sequencing worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on the correct order of the hosting procedure.","Recap: domain > files > DNS > test"]],"questions":["What does 'web hosting' provide to a website owner?","List the first and last steps in the typical web hosting procedure.","Why is testing the live site an important final step?"],"summary":"Web hosting provides the server infrastructure to make a website live; following a clear procedure - from choosing a host to testing the final site - ensures a smooth launch.","assignment":"Write a step-by-step guide (5-6 steps) describing how to host a simple website, based on what was covered in class.","next":"Online tools - Google Forms, Google Sheets, Google Drive, Google Classroom, Moodle, Chamilo, Open edX, Totara Learn, Canvas."},"50":{"no":50,"topic":"Online Tools - Google Forms, Sheets, Drive, Classroom, Moodle, Chamilo, Open edX, Totara Learn, Canvas","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"10 (Cloud Computing)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"31 March 2027 to 2 April 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify common online tools used for forms, collaboration and e-learning.","Explain the basic purpose of each listed tool."],"materials":["Computer with internet access","Sample accounts on Google Forms/Classroom (if available)"],"review":"Recall the web hosting procedure covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"These online tools are widely used in institutes and workplaces for surveys, collaboration and training - valuable digital literacy for a DTP operator.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Google Forms (surveys/quizzes), Google Sheets (collaborative spreadsheets), Google Drive (cloud storage/sharing), Google Classroom (course management).","Google Workspace tools overview"],["Presentation / Development","Moodle, Chamilo, Open edX, Totara Learn and Canvas are Learning Management Systems (LMS) used by institutes for delivering and tracking online courses.","LMS platforms comparison chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees create a simple sample form using Google Forms and explore a demo LMS course page (if available).","Google Forms creation demo"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz matching each tool to its primary function.","Recap: Forms=surveys, Classroom/LMS=courses"]],"questions":["What is Google Forms typically used for?","Name two Learning Management Systems (LMS) discussed in this lesson.","How might a DTP institute use Google Classroom or an LMS?"],"summary":"A range of online tools - from Google's collaboration suite to dedicated Learning Management Systems - support surveys, file sharing and structured online course delivery.","assignment":"Create a simple 3-question Google Form on any topic and share the link.","next":"Categories of content that require publishing; classify all requirements of the publication content."},"51":{"no":51,"topic":"Categories of Content That Require Publishing; Classify All Requirements of Publication Content","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"11 (Publishing Requirements)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"3 April 2027 to 6 April 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["List the different categories of content that require publishing.","Classify the general requirements involved in publishing content."],"materials":["White board & marker","Sample publication examples (magazine, brochure, book)"],"review":"Recall the online tools and LMS platforms covered in the previous unit.","motivation":"Understanding content categories and requirements is the foundation of the publishing-process units ahead.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Content categories: text documents, images/graphics, multimedia, structured data (tables/forms) - each requiring different publishing treatment.","Content category examples chart"],["Presentation / Development","Publishing requirements: content accuracy, correct formatting, appropriate media, brand/style compliance, and approval sign-off before release.","Requirements checklist"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees classify sample content pieces (brochure text, product photo, price table) by category and list their key requirements.","Classification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on content categories and their typical requirements.","Recap: text, image, multimedia, data categories"]],"questions":["Name three categories of content that may require publishing.","Why does each content category have different publishing requirements?","What is one common requirement across nearly all publication content?"],"summary":"Publishing content spans several categories - text, images, multimedia and data - each with its own accuracy, formatting and approval requirements.","assignment":"List four types of content your institute might publish and one specific requirement for each.","next":"Different kinds of publishing contents; types of printing technology (Letterpress, Offset lithography, Screen printing, Gravure, Flexography)."},"52":{"no":52,"topic":"Different Kinds of Publishing Content; Types of Printing Technology (Letterpress, Offset Lithography, Screen Printing, Gravure, Flexography)","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"11 (Publishing Requirements)","time":"4 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"7 April 2027 to 12 April 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify different kinds of publishing content (books, magazines, packaging, digital).","Describe the major types of printing technology and their typical uses."],"materials":["White board & marker","Sample printed materials (if available) showing different print techniques"],"review":"Recall the content categories and requirements covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Knowing printing technologies helps a DTP operator prepare files correctly for the specific print method a client will use.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Publishing content types: books, magazines, newspapers, packaging, brochures, digital/web publications.","Publishing content type examples"],["Presentation / Development","Printing technologies: Letterpress (raised type, traditional), Offset Lithography (most common for books/magazines), Screen Printing (bold colours on fabric/signage), Gravure (high-volume packaging), Flexography (labels/packaging).","Printing technology comparison chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees match sample printed items (a magazine, a t-shirt design, a food package) to the likely printing technology used.","Matching worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on which printing technology suits which output type.","Recap: Offset=books/magazines, Gravure=packaging"]],"questions":["Which printing technology is most commonly used for books and magazines?","Name one printing technology suited to packaging.","What distinguishes Screen Printing from Offset Lithography?"],"summary":"Different publishing content types require matching printing technologies - from traditional letterpress to modern offset, screen, gravure and flexography - each suited to specific outputs.","assignment":"Research and note which printing technology would best suit: (a) a wedding invitation, (b) a cereal box, (c) a t-shirt design.","next":"Digital printing technology; discuss issues, if any, concerning requirements."},"53":{"no":53,"topic":"Digital Printing Technology; Discuss Issues Concerning Requirements","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"11 (Publishing Requirements)","time":"4 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"13 April 2027 to 17 April 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain digital printing technology and how it differs from traditional print methods.","Discuss common issues that arise concerning publication requirements."],"materials":["White board & marker","Sample digitally printed materials"],"review":"Recall the traditional printing technologies covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Digital printing is increasingly common for short runs and personalisation - directly relevant to modern DTP work.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Digital printing prints directly from a digital file without plates, ideal for short runs, quick turnaround and personalisation (e.g. variable data printing).","Digital vs traditional print comparison"],["Presentation / Development","Common requirement issues: unclear specifications, missing approvals, incorrect colour profiles, last-minute content changes, mismatched file formats.","Common issues checklist"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees discuss a scenario involving a requirement issue in a digital print job and suggest how it could have been avoided.","Scenario discussion worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on the advantages of digital printing and common requirement issues.","Recap: short runs, personalisation, common pitfalls"]],"questions":["What is one key advantage of digital printing over offset printing for short runs?","Name one common issue that can arise from unclear publication requirements.","What does 'variable data printing' allow you to do?"],"summary":"Digital printing offers speed and personalisation advantages for short runs, but clear requirement communication remains essential to avoid costly errors.","assignment":"List three common causes of requirement-related issues in a publishing project and one way to prevent each.","next":"Summarize the consequences of publishing incorrect versions of content."},"54":{"no":54,"topic":"Summarise the Consequences of Publishing Incorrect Versions of Content","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"11 (Publishing Requirements)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"20 April 2027 to 22 April 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Summarise the range of consequences that can result from publishing incorrect content.","Relate these consequences to real-world organisational impact."],"materials":["White board & marker","Case studies of publishing errors"],"review":"Recall the digital printing technology and requirement issues covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"This reinforces why accuracy and version control (covered practically in this unit) matter for every publication.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Consequences of incorrect publishing include misinformation, financial loss, legal liability and damaged credibility.","Consequences overview chart"],["Presentation / Development","Real-world examples: a wrong price printed on packaging, an outdated policy published online, a typo in a legal document - each with real cost.","Case study examples"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees summarise a given case study's consequences in their own words.","Case study summary exercise"],["Consolidation / Checking","Group discussion: which consequence type (financial/legal/reputational) is hardest to recover from, and why?","Recap: reputational damage often longest-lasting"]],"questions":["Name two types of consequences an organisation may face from publishing incorrect content.","Why can reputational damage be harder to recover from than financial loss?","Give a real or hypothetical example of a costly publishing error."],"summary":"Publishing incorrect content can lead to financial, legal and reputational consequences - reinforcing the importance of the verification and quality-control practices covered in this unit.","assignment":"Write a 100-150 word summary of the consequences of publishing incorrect content, using one example.","next":"Summarize the need for publishing content. Classify the media recommended for publishing content."},"55":{"no":55,"topic":"Need for Publishing Content; Classify Media Recommended for Publishing Content","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"11 (Publishing Requirements)","time":"4 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"23 April 2027 to 27 April 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Explain why organisations need to publish content.","Classify the different media types recommended for publishing content."],"materials":["White board & marker","Examples of content published across different media"],"review":"Recall the consequences of publishing incorrect content covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"Understanding why and where to publish content connects the technical DTP skills learned so far to real business purposes.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Organisations publish content to inform, market, educate, comply with regulations, and engage audiences.","Reasons for publishing content chart"],["Presentation / Development","Media types: print (brochures/books), digital/web (websites/PDFs), social media, email newsletters, video/multimedia - each suited to different audiences and purposes.","Media type comparison chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees match a given publishing goal (e.g. reach young audience quickly) to the most suitable media type.","Matching worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz on reasons for publishing and matching goals to media.","Recap: print=durable, digital=fast/wide reach"]],"questions":["Name two reasons an organisation might need to publish content.","Which media type would best reach a young audience quickly, and why?","Name one advantage print media has over digital media."],"summary":"Organisations publish content for varied purposes - information, marketing, compliance - and choosing the right media (print, digital, social) is key to reaching the intended audience effectively.","assignment":"Choose a publishing goal (e.g. announcing an event) and recommend the best media type with reasons.","next":"Compare various methods of publishing content. Prepare a list of publishing requirements obtained from the requester."},"56":{"no":56,"topic":"Compare Methods of Publishing Content; Prepare a List of Publishing Requirements from the Requester","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"11 (Publishing Requirements)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"28 April 2027 to 30 April 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Compare different methods used to publish content.","Prepare a structured list of publishing requirements based on a requester's needs."],"materials":["White board & marker","Sample requester brief"],"review":"Recall the media types recommended for publishing content, covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"This lesson ties directly into the demonstration work on gathering and documenting publication requirements.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Publishing methods vary: manual print production, automated digital publishing (CMS/templates), on-demand printing, and multi-channel simultaneous publishing.","Publishing methods comparison chart"],["Presentation / Development","Steps to prepare a requirements list from a requester: interview/brief review, note content type, format, deadline, audience and approval process.","Requirements list template"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees review a sample requester brief and extract a structured requirements list from it.","Sample brief extraction exercise"],["Consolidation / Checking","Group review of extracted requirements lists for completeness.","Recap: content, format, deadline, audience, approval"]],"questions":["Name two different methods of publishing content.","What five key pieces of information should a publishing requirements list typically capture?","Why is it useful to compare publishing methods before starting a project?"],"summary":"Comparing publishing methods and systematically capturing a requester's needs into a clear requirements list sets a publishing project up for success.","assignment":"Given a sample requester brief, prepare a structured list of at least 5 publishing requirements.","next":"Tools for Creating and Managing Multilingual Content; What is Binding, types of binding and process."},"57":{"no":57,"topic":"Tools for Creating and Managing Multilingual Content; What is Binding, Types of Binding and Process","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"12 (Bilingual Software / Binding)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"3 May 2027 to 5 May 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Identify tools used for creating and managing multilingual content.","Explain the concept of binding and describe common binding types."],"materials":["Computer with internet access and Unicode font support","Sample bound publications showing different binding types"],"review":"Recall the publishing requirements and methods covered in the previous unit.","motivation":"Multilingual content tools and binding knowledge are essential for producing finished, distributable publications in India's diverse-language markets.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Tools for multilingual content: email, FTP, Notepad, Unicode fonts, MS Office, PDF reader/creator, Adobe Creative Cloud, translation memory, WordPress and social media.","Multilingual tools list chart"],["Presentation / Development","Binding is the process of assembling and securing printed pages within a cover. Common types: Sewn, Glued/Perfect, PUR-glued, Lay-flat, Spiral, Wire-O and Saddle-stitched binding.","Binding types comparison chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees identify the binding type used in sample bound publications (a magazine, a notebook, a paperback book).","Binding identification worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Quiz matching tools to multilingual tasks, and binding types to their typical use.","Recap: Unicode fonts=multilingual text, Saddle-stitch=magazines"]],"questions":["Why are Unicode fonts important when working with multilingual content?","Name two types of binding used in publishing.","Which binding type is most commonly used for thin magazines?"],"summary":"Managing multilingual content requires the right software/font tools, while binding - in its various forms - determines how finished printed pages are assembled into a usable publication.","assignment":"Examine three bound items you have at home/institute and identify each one's binding type.","next":"Recap of Sewn, Glued/Perfect, PUR-glued, Lay-flat, Spiral, Wire-O and Saddle-stitched binding processes."},"58":{"no":58,"topic":"Binding Types in Detail - Sewn, Glued/Perfect, PUR-Glued, Lay-Flat, Spiral, Wire-O and Saddle-Stitched Binding","instructor":"Durgesh Dnyandev Kadam (Craft Instructor, DTP Trade)","trade":"Desktop Publishing Operator (DTPO)","batch":"1st Year DTPO, Academic Session 2026-27","unit":"12 (Bilingual Software / Binding)","time":"3 working day(s) (Theory Period)","proposed":"6 May 2027 to 10 May 2027","conducted":"","objectives":["Describe the process and characteristics of each major binding type in detail.","Recommend an appropriate binding type for a given publication scenario."],"materials":["Sample bound publications of each type (if available)","White board & marker"],"review":"Recall the overview of multilingual tools and binding types covered in the previous lesson.","motivation":"This final lesson consolidates practical binding knowledge trainees will apply when specifying finishing requirements for real publishing jobs.","steps":[["Preparation / Introduction","Sewn binding: pages sewn together in sections - strong and durable, used for hardcover books. Glued/Perfect binding: pages glued at the spine - common for paperbacks.","Sewn vs Perfect binding diagram"],["Presentation / Development","PUR-glued binding: a stronger, flexible glue variant of perfect binding. Lay-flat binding: allows a book to lie fully open - used for photo books. Spiral and Wire-O: coil/wire through punched holes, allowing 360-degree page turning - used for manuals/notebooks. Saddle-stitched: staples through the fold - used for thin booklets/magazines.","Binding types visual reference chart"],["Application / Recapitulation","Trainees recommend the best binding type for given scenarios (a cookbook that must lie flat, a 16-page event program, a spiral-bound training manual).","Scenario-based recommendation worksheet"],["Consolidation / Checking","Final quiz reviewing all binding types covered across both lessons.","Recap: full binding types comparison chart"]],"questions":["Which binding type would you recommend for a cookbook that needs to lie flat while in use?","What distinguishes PUR-glued binding from standard perfect binding?","Which binding type allows pages to be turned a full 360 degrees?"],"summary":"Each binding type - from durable sewn binding to flexible spiral/Wire-O and economical saddle-stitching - suits different publication needs; recommending the right one completes a publication's production specification.","assignment":"For three different publication types (a textbook, a magazine, a training manual), recommend and justify a suitable binding type.","next":"(Theory syllabus complete - proceed to revision, project work and examination preparation.)"}};
